WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:06.150
In 1992, seven of the hottest artists in comics
left Marvel to form Image Comics, and before

00:00:06.150 --> 00:00:08.400
long, one of those artists had a hit on their
hands.

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:11.280
Todd McFarlane’s Spawn quickly became a
phenomenon.

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:16.049
The story of a black ops soldier killed in
action and resurrected as a supernatural warrior

00:00:16.049 --> 00:00:21.320
meant to serve the will of Hell, Spawn has
turned into a media empire, with almost 300

00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:27.130
issues of his own comic, numerous spin-offs,
video games, an HBO animated series, a live-action

00:00:27.130 --> 00:00:28.420
movie, and more.

00:00:28.420 --> 00:00:31.160
"Welcome to my world!

00:00:31.160 --> 00:00:35.500
From Todd McFarlane's Spawn, She-Spawn!"

00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:39.630
From the fairly public legal battles to the
character’s surprising secret origin, here

00:00:39.630 --> 00:00:43.760
are the stories about Spawn that you won’t
find on the comic page.

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:45.450
Sweet Sixteen

00:00:45.450 --> 00:00:49.230
When Image launched with all-new characters,
McFarlane didn't have to think long to figure

00:00:49.230 --> 00:00:50.540
out what he wanted to do.

00:00:50.540 --> 00:00:55.510
He’d been waiting since 1977 to tell the
story of Spawn, a character he created when

00:00:55.510 --> 00:01:00.360
he was 16 years old, and the dawn of Image
Comics gave him the opportunity to bring him

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:03.440
to life… or undeath, depending on how you
look at it.

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:07.550
To back up his claims of creating Spawn as
a teenager, McFarlane took to Facebook in

00:01:07.550 --> 00:01:12.440
October of 2017 to post what he said was one
of his first drawings of the character.

00:01:12.440 --> 00:01:16.490
He’s not quite the version we know today,
with a different color scheme and what appears

00:01:16.490 --> 00:01:21.440
to be a cartoony sidekick, but it’s clearly
a prototype version of Al Simmons’ hell-spawned

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:22.470
alter-ego.

00:01:22.470 --> 00:01:23.870
The big difference?

00:01:23.870 --> 00:01:28.270
McFarlane said he originally envisioned Spawn
as being from space rather than the underworld.

00:01:28.270 --> 00:01:31.510
"I dub three SPAWN!

00:01:31.510 --> 00:01:34.760
General of hell's army!"

00:01:34.760 --> 00:01:36.390
So many powers

00:01:36.390 --> 00:01:40.440
If you don't know much about Spawn, your first
instinct might be to ask, "What powers does

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:41.440
he have?"

00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:45.460
It’s a simple question, and even for an
infamously powerful character like Superman,

00:01:45.460 --> 00:01:48.960
you can rattle off a list of all of his major
abilities pretty quickly.

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:51.720
With Spawn, however, we could be here all
day.

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:55.210
He's got the standard-issue enhanced strength
and durability that you’d expect from a

00:01:55.210 --> 00:01:58.159
superhero, but that’s barely scratching
the surface.

00:01:58.159 --> 00:02:03.840
As of Spawn #4, he was blasting glowing “necroplasm”
out of his hands, and surviving the violent

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:06.060
loss of limbs and major organs.

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:11.340
In later stories, he’s seen teleporting,
resurrecting the dead, breathing fire, stopping

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:15.110
time, animating chains, and plenty of other
wild displays of power.

00:02:15.110 --> 00:02:18.730
It’s enough to make you wonder if he could
ever be threatened enough to keep readers

00:02:18.730 --> 00:02:22.970
interested, but with the comic still going
strong after 25 years, it doesn't seem his

00:02:22.970 --> 00:02:26.450
Swiss Army knife collection of powers has
hurt his appeal.

00:02:26.450 --> 00:02:28.370
A landmark in diversity

00:02:28.370 --> 00:02:32.220
Before his death and resurrection, Spawn was
Al Simmons, a black man.

00:02:32.220 --> 00:02:36.200
You wouldn’t know that from looking at his
full-body costume, or even the rotted corpse

00:02:36.200 --> 00:02:39.170
underneath, but that’s also on purpose.

00:02:39.170 --> 00:02:42.830
McFarlane has said on numerous occasions that
his choice to make Simmons African-American

00:02:42.830 --> 00:02:47.300
was in part because of a story he never got
to tell while he was working on Spider-Man.

00:02:47.300 --> 00:02:51.400
The hypothetical scene would have Spider-Man
save a white man from a black criminal, only

00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:54.740
to have the man he saved use a racial slur
to describe the crook.

00:02:54.740 --> 00:02:59.360
“Because he's covered from head to toe,
unlike Batman, he's covered from head to toe,

00:02:59.360 --> 00:03:04.850
and go, 'what color do you think I am under
this mask?'"

00:03:04.850 --> 00:03:09.470
The 1997 Spawn movie may not have been met
with critical acclaim for obvious reasons…

00:03:09.470 --> 00:03:12.050
"Easy with that face, I'm eating.

00:03:12.050 --> 00:03:14.480
Opps, a wet one."

00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:19.500
...or even record-breaking ticket sales, but
McFarlane’s choice did lead to a landmark

00:03:19.500 --> 00:03:23.850
for superhero movies: Michael Jai White wound
up being the first African-American actor

00:03:23.850 --> 00:03:29.050
to play a major comic book superhero, though
just barely: Spawn's theatrical release preceded

00:03:29.050 --> 00:03:32.130
the release of Steel, starring Shaquille O'Neal,
by only 14 days.

00:03:32.130 --> 00:03:38.560
"With a will of iron, a heart of gold, and
a body of steel…"

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:39.760
"It's hammer time."

00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:41.500
Spawn goes digital

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:46.980
By leaving Marvel at the height of his popularity,
co-founding a successful independent publisher,

00:03:46.989 --> 00:03:51.020
and using a single character’s success to
launch a cross-media empire, it’s easy to

00:03:51.020 --> 00:03:54.970
argue Todd McFarlane spent a lot of time on
the bleeding edge of the comics industry.

00:03:54.970 --> 00:03:58.680
That’s why it's so surprising that when
it came to digital publishing, he was one

00:03:58.680 --> 00:04:01.260
of the last creators to get on board.

00:04:01.260 --> 00:04:04.680
McFarlane told the Washington Post that holding
off from digital publishing was his way of

00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:08.629
staying loyal to brick-and-mortar comic book
shops by keeping Spawn on the shelves and

00:04:08.629 --> 00:04:09.760
off the tablets.

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:12.200
Eventually, though, the convenience of digital
won out.

00:04:12.200 --> 00:04:18.969
At the release of Spawn #250 in 2015, the
entire library became available online.

00:04:18.969 --> 00:04:20.849
The unreleased crossover

00:04:20.849 --> 00:04:25.009
With his overwhelming popularity in the ‘90s,
it’s not surprising that Spawn starred in

00:04:25.009 --> 00:04:29.599
plenty of crossovers, including two notable
stories where he teamed up with Batman.

00:04:29.599 --> 00:04:34.090
The first was drawn by McFarlane with a script
by Dark Knight Returns creator Frank Miller,

00:04:34.090 --> 00:04:38.199
which actually left literal scars on Spawn
that carried over to his own comic.

00:04:38.199 --> 00:04:43.130
The second was Batman/Spawn: War Devil, and
in 2015, McFarlane revealed that there were

00:04:43.130 --> 00:04:48.169
originally plans to complete a Batman/Spawn
trilogy that never came to pass.

00:04:48.169 --> 00:04:51.970
McFarlane signed on to write and ink while
artist Greg Capullo, who got his start on

00:04:51.970 --> 00:04:56.280
Spawn before moving to an epic five-year run
on Batman, would have drawn.

00:04:56.280 --> 00:05:00.370
McFarlane didn't say precisely why the crossover
died on the vine, only that it was for a variety

00:05:00.370 --> 00:05:04.039
of reasons which he claimed most of the responsibility
for.

00:05:04.039 --> 00:05:06.020
The war over Angela

00:05:06.020 --> 00:05:09.789
During the first year of Spawn, McFarlane
invited a number of well-known comic book

00:05:09.789 --> 00:05:14.300
writers to write issues of the series, including
notable writer Neil Gaiman.

00:05:14.300 --> 00:05:19.389
In Spawn #9, Gaiman introduced an angelic
warrior named Angela and the Medieval Spawn,

00:05:19.389 --> 00:05:22.569
who would wind up being very important as
Spawn’s saga went on.

00:05:22.569 --> 00:05:26.379
Gaiman felt he deserved more compensation
for his contributions, and after a failed

00:05:26.379 --> 00:05:30.990
negotiation involving the rights to another
character, Gaiman filed suit against McFarlane

00:05:30.990 --> 00:05:32.240
in 2002.

00:05:32.240 --> 00:05:34.659
Ten years later, the two creators finally
settled.

00:05:34.659 --> 00:05:38.830
The details weren’t released to the public,
but Gaiman was granted ownership of Angela.

00:05:38.830 --> 00:05:44.309
In 2013, in a move that surprised many readers,
he brought her into the Marvel Universe, where

00:05:44.309 --> 00:05:47.119
she was eventually revealed to be Thor’s
sister.

00:05:47.119 --> 00:05:50.439
McFarlane's diplomatic response to the news
was that "Whatever Neil chooses to do with

00:05:50.439 --> 00:05:54.210
something that he owns is at his complete
and utter discretion."

00:05:54.210 --> 00:05:56.129
Taking back the reins

00:05:56.129 --> 00:06:00.819
Over 20 years after his first film, Spawn
is returning to the big screen in a live-action

00:06:00.819 --> 00:06:06.539
reboot scheduled for production in 2018, and
this time, McFarlane will be writing and directing.

00:06:06.539 --> 00:06:11.259
He’s made it clear he doesn't intend for
it to be anything like the 1997 movie, and

00:06:11.259 --> 00:06:14.039
even claims that it won’t be a superhero
film either.

00:06:14.039 --> 00:06:18.599
Instead, he frequently compares it to Steven
Spielberg’s Jaws, with Spawn in the role

00:06:18.599 --> 00:06:19.599
of the shark.

00:06:19.599 --> 00:06:22.930
The story will be told through the eyes of
the normal people around him, and will skip

00:06:22.930 --> 00:06:25.080
over the origin story completely.

00:06:25.080 --> 00:06:28.470
As McFarlane says, "Did they tell me why the
shark was so damn big?

00:06:28.470 --> 00:06:29.470
No!

00:06:29.470 --> 00:06:30.750
Did it matter to me?

00:06:30.750 --> 00:06:31.750
No!”

00:06:31.750 --> 00:06:33.980
Will it be as ridiculous as the first?

00:06:35.500 --> 00:06:39.960
"Noooo!"

00:06:39.960 --> 00:06:42.900
"Oh come on, you scream like a girl!"

00:06:42.909 --> 00:06:44.029
Thanks for watching!

00:06:44.029 --> 00:06:46.779
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:06:46.779 --> 00:06:48.840
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

